#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""oe-repair -- put Claude Code's settings back when a hook entry points at nothing.

WHY THIS FILE IS ALONE
----------------------
Claude Code runs a PreToolUse hook before EVERY tool call. Delete the script a
registered PreToolUse hook names and every Bash, Read and Write in every session
on the machine fails the same way:

    PreToolUse:Bash hook error: ... can not open file '.../hooks/pre_tool_use.py'

The assistant cannot read settings.json to see the problem, cannot recreate the
script, and cannot repair itself, because reading and writing are the things
that broke. Getting out of that state takes a shell command typed by a human.

So the recovery path has to live OUTSIDE Claude Code, and it has to survive the
state that produced it. install.py cannot do this job -- it does
`from oe import checklist` at module scope, so it dies with ModuleNotFoundError
the moment oe/ is missing, and whatever removed a hook script may well have
removed oe/ modules too. This file therefore imports NOTHING from oe/. Standard
library only, one file, no package, no install, no PATH entry, no virtualenv:

    python3 /path/to/overwatch-enforcer/bin/oe-repair

If the entire oe/ directory were deleted, this still runs and still repairs the
settings. That property is the point of the file, and it is why there is some
duplication here (colour helpers, backup naming, the untouched-keys check) that
looks like it belongs in a shared module. It does not. A shared module is one
more file that can be missing at the worst moment.

WHY THIS BUILD DOES NOT WRITE
----------------------------
v1.0.0 diagnoses and stops. The repair path is held back for one release
because it can remove a registration that is WORKING:

  * `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` and relative hook paths are resolved against
    whichever directory you happen to be standing in, so a hook that works in
    its own project reads as missing from anywhere else;
  * a wrapper command -- `uv run x.py`, `env A=1 python3 x.py`, `npx tsx x.ts`
    -- has its SUBCOMMAND read as the script name, so the real script is never
    looked for and a working entry is reported broken;
  * a symlinked settings.json is replaced by a regular file, severing the link
    to wherever the real one lives.

Deleting a live PreToolUse registration stops every tool call in every session
on this machine. That is a worse outcome than the fault being repaired, and it
is not a hypothetical -- it is why this tool exists. Until those three are
fixed, the plan is printed and applied by hand: the changes are small and every
line is quoted.

WHAT IT WILL NOT DO
-------------------
It never writes a hook file, never creates a no-op hook, and never touches
anything under hooks/. Writing a no-op into a registered PreToolUse hook is
indistinguishable from disabling the tool-call security gate -- that is a worse
outcome than a broken hook, and it is correctly refused elsewhere. The only
repair here is REMOVING a registration that points at nothing.

It never rewrites a file it cannot parse. A settings.json with a missing comma
is broken, but it is also the only copy of the user's configuration, and a
rewrite we cannot prove preserved it is how a fixable problem becomes a lost
one. Unparseable means: report the line and column, quote the line, stop.

It never edits managed/policy settings. Those belong to an administrator; they
are found, read, and reported, and that is all.

CONTRACT
--------
    DIAGNOSE ONLY        this build never writes. See below.
    --yes                accepted and refused, with the reason
    --all                also removes another tool's broken entries (never the
                         default: silently deleting a third-party integration is
                         worse than leaving it and saying so)
    --root DIR           which checkout counts as "ours", when this file has
                         been copied out of one
    --settings FILE      check exactly one file, for rehearsing on a copy
    --json               the same verdict on stdout, machine readable
    exit 0               nothing broken, or everything broken was repaired
    exit 1               something is broken and still unrepaired
    exit 2               this tool could not run (bad arguments)

WHAT IT REMOVES, AND WHAT IT ONLY REPORTS
-----------------------------------------
Removes: a hook or statusLine entry whose script is not there, whose
interpreter is not there, or whose shape is not something Claude Code can run
at all. Those are unambiguous -- the entry cannot work, and the file it names
is gone.

Reports only: a script that exists but is not executable, or has no shebang and
no interpreter in front of it. The fix there is one chmod or one line, and
deleting the registration would throw away a working configuration to solve it.
Also reports only: a command with a pipe, a variable this shell cannot expand,
or inline `-c` code -- what those run cannot be decided by looking at them, and
a wrong guess here deletes something that worked.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile

# Deliberately no `from typing import ...` beyond what runs on an old 3.x, and
# deliberately no third-party anything. The Python that runs this is whatever
# the user's panicking shell finds first.

VERSION = 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# where things are
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: The parent of bin/ when this file is where it ships, and the directory
#: holding it otherwise -- a copy dropped somewhere else still runs, it just
#: cannot attribute anything to an install it is not inside. Never a literal:
#: a hardcoded path is right on exactly one machine, and this file exists to be
#: run on other people's.
def _default_root():
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    if os.path.basename(here) == "bin":
        return os.path.dirname(here)
    return here


def _claude_home():
    """~/.claude, honouring Claude Code's own CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR override."""
    env = str(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or "").strip()
    if env:
        return os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(env))
    return os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude"))


#: Read out of the 2.1.267 binary rather than remembered: the policy directory
#: is chosen by platform (macos -> /Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode,
#: windows -> C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode, everything else -> /etc/claude-code)
#: and the files inside it are managed-settings.json plus a managed-settings.d
#: drop-in directory of *.json. WSL reads the Windows copy under /mnt/c AS WELL
#: AS the Linux one, which is why both are listed rather than one or the other.
def _policy_dirs():
    dirs = []
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        dirs.append("/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode")
    elif os.name == "nt":
        dirs.append(r"C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode")
    else:
        dirs.append("/etc/claude-code")
        if os.environ.get("WSL_DISTRO_NAME") or os.path.exists("/proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/WSLInterop"):
            dirs.append("/mnt/c/Program Files/ClaudeCode")
    return dirs


def _project_dir(explicit):
    """The directory Claude Code would treat as this session's project root.

    Hooks get CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR in the environment; from a bare shell -- which
    is where somebody reading this is -- fall back to the nearest ancestor of
    the cwd carrying a .claude/ directory, because that is where a
    project-scoped settings file lives. ~/.claude is skipped: treating $HOME as
    a project would read the user settings file twice under two labels.
    """
    if explicit:
        return os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(explicit))
    env = str(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or "").strip()
    if env and os.path.isdir(env):
        return os.path.abspath(env)
    try:
        here = os.path.abspath(os.getcwd())
    except Exception:
        return None
    home = _claude_home()
    while True:
        marker = os.path.join(here, ".claude")
        if os.path.isdir(marker) and os.path.abspath(marker) != home:
            return here
        parent = os.path.dirname(here)
        if parent == here:
            return None
        here = parent


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything printed here is read by somebody whose tooling just stopped
# working, so every line either says what is wrong or says what to do about it.
# Nothing is decorative.

_QUIET = False        # --json: the only thing on stdout may be the JSON


def _colour():
    try:
        return bool(sys.stdout.isatty()) and not os.environ.get("NO_COLOR")
    except Exception:
        return False


def c(text, code):
    return "\x1b[%sm%s\x1b[0m" % (code, text) if code and _colour() else text


def _emit(stream, text):
    """print() that cannot itself become the failure.

    On a full disk the write we are REPORTING is not the only one that fails;
    print() fails too, and an OSError out of fail() would replace 'write failed,
    rolled back' with a traceback and skip the rollback. The reporting path has
    to survive the condition it exists to report.
    """
    try:
        print(text, file=stream)
        stream.flush()
    except Exception:
        pass


def say(text=""):
    if not _QUIET:
        _emit(sys.stdout, text)


def warn(text):
    try:
        sys.stdout.flush()
    except Exception:
        pass
    _emit(sys.stderr, c("  ! " + text, "33"))


def fail(text):
    try:
        sys.stdout.flush()
    except Exception:
        pass
    _emit(sys.stderr, c("  x " + text, "31;1"))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# reading a settings file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class SettingsFile(object):
    """One settings file: where it is, whether it can be read, and what is in it.

    `state` is the only thing the rest of the file branches on:
        missing        not there, which for every scope but the user one is normal
        unreadable     there, but the OS would not hand it over
        unparseable    there, but not JSON -- NOT repairable, see repair()
        not-an-object  valid JSON that is a list or a string, so not settings
        ok             a dict we can walk
    """

    def __init__(self, scope, path, writable=True):
        self.scope = scope
        self.path = path
        self.writable = writable          # False for managed/policy files
        self.state = "missing"
        self.raw = b""                    # exactly the bytes load() read
        self.text = ""
        self.data = None
        self.error = None                 # dict for --json, also drives the report
        self.findings = []
        # Diagnosable but NOT rewritable: the file parses, so every finding in
        # it is real and worth printing, but something in it cannot survive a
        # json.dumps round trip. See _rewrite_blocker(). None means "fine".
        self.rewrite_block = None

    @property
    def rewritable(self):
        return self.rewrite_block is None

    # -- read -------------------------------------------------------------
    def load(self):
        if not os.path.exists(self.path):
            self.state = "missing"
            return
        try:
            with open(self.path, "rb") as handle:
                raw = self.raw = handle.read()
        except Exception as exc:
            self.state = "unreadable"
            self.error = {"kind": "unreadable",
                          "message": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)}
            return
        try:
            self.text = raw.decode("utf-8")
        except Exception as exc:
            self.state = "unreadable"
            self.error = {"kind": "not-utf8",
                          "message": "not valid UTF-8: %s" % exc}
            return
        literals = []
        try:
            # object_pairs_hook is not needed -- dict has preserved insertion
            # order since 3.7 -- but the ORDER is load-bearing here (a
            # reordered settings.json is its own kind of damage), so it is
            # stated rather than assumed.
            #
            # parse_constant records the bare tokens NaN / Infinity / -Infinity.
            # Python's json accepts all three; Node's JSON.parse -- which is
            # what Claude Code reads settings with -- rejects them. We have to
            # know they were there, because we must not hand them back.
            self.data = json.loads(self.text, object_pairs_hook=_ordered,
                                   parse_constant=lambda name: literals.append(name)
                                   or _JSON_CONSTANTS.get(name))
        except ValueError as exc:
            self.state = "unparseable"
            self.error = _parse_error(self.text, exc)
            return
        if not isinstance(self.data, dict):
            self.state = "not-an-object"
            self.error = {"kind": "not-an-object",
                          "message": "the top level is a %s, and Claude Code's "
                                     "settings must be a JSON object"
                                     % type(self.data).__name__}
            return
        self.state = "ok"
        self.rewrite_block = _rewrite_blocker(self.data, literals)


_JSON_CONSTANTS = {"NaN": float("nan"),
                   "Infinity": float("inf"),
                   "-Infinity": float("-inf")}


def _nonfinite_locations(value, trail=""):
    """Every path in the document holding a float json.dumps cannot write.

    Iterative rather than recursive: a settings.json nested a thousand deep is
    valid JSON, and a rescue tool must not be the thing that raises
    RecursionError on it.
    """
    found = []
    stack = [(trail or "(root)", value)]
    while stack:
        where, node = stack.pop()
        if isinstance(node, float):
            if node != node or node in (float("inf"), float("-inf")):
                found.append(where)
        elif isinstance(node, dict):
            for key, sub in node.items():
                stack.append(("%s.%s" % (where, key), sub))
        elif isinstance(node, list):
            for index, sub in enumerate(node):
                stack.append(("%s[%d]" % (where, index), sub))
    return sorted(found)


def _rewrite_blocker(data, literals):
    """None when this document can be written back safely; else why it cannot.

    THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS. Python's json reads `1e999` as `inf` and writes
    it back as the bare token `Infinity`. Python then reads `Infinity` again
    quite happily -- so every check this tool ran passed, it printed "verified
    on disk" and "repaired", and exited 0 over a settings.json that Node, and
    therefore Claude Code, can no longer parse. One dead hook entry went in and
    an unloadable configuration came out, which is the precise outcome this
    file exists to prevent. The guard is here rather than only at the writer
    because the honest answer is to say so BEFORE taking a backup and touching
    anything.

    Two shapes, and they are not the same problem:

      * a NaN / Infinity / -Infinity LITERAL. Claude Code cannot read the file
        as it stands -- this is already broken, and not in a way a hook removal
        would fix.
      * a finite literal that OVERFLOWS to infinity, `1e999`. Node reads this
        file perfectly well (verified: JSON.parse gives Infinity). It is only
        our rewrite that would break it, so leaving it alone is the whole fix.
    """
    if literals:
        return {"kind": "json5-constant",
                "tokens": sorted(set(literals)),
                "message": "it contains the bare token(s) %s. Python's JSON "
                           "reader accepts those; the JSON parser Claude Code "
                           "uses does not, so this file is already unreadable "
                           "to it -- and rewriting it would not change that."
                           % ", ".join(sorted(set(literals)))}
    spots = _nonfinite_locations(data)
    if spots:
        return {"kind": "overflowing-number",
                "tokens": spots,
                "message": "the number at %s is too large for JSON to hold "
                           "(it reads as infinity). Claude Code can read this "
                           "file as it stands, but anything that rewrote it "
                           "would have to spell that number `Infinity`, which "
                           "Claude Code cannot read. So it is left alone."
                           % ", ".join(spots[:3])}
    return None


def _ordered(pairs):
    """json's object hook. A later duplicate key wins, exactly as json does by
    default, and the first occurrence keeps its position -- so a file with a
    repeated key round-trips to the same document json handed us."""
    out = {}
    for key, value in pairs:
        out[key] = value
    return out


def _parse_error(text, exc):
    """A JSON syntax error somebody can actually act on.

    A user staring at `Expecting ',' delimiter: line 14 column 3 (char 402)` in
    a stack trace does not find the missing comma. The line itself, with a caret
    under the column, does.
    """
    info = {"kind": "unparseable",
            "message": str(getattr(exc, "msg", None) or exc),
            "line": getattr(exc, "lineno", None),
            "column": getattr(exc, "colno", None),
            "source": None}
    lines = text.splitlines()
    line_no = info["line"]
    if isinstance(line_no, int) and 1 <= line_no <= len(lines):
        info["source"] = lines[line_no - 1]
    if not text.strip():
        # A genuinely empty settings.json is the commonest shape of this and the
        # least obvious: it is not "settings with nothing in them", it is a
        # syntax error, and the fix is two characters.
        info["empty"] = True
    return info


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# reading a hook command
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Claude Code hook command is a shell string: "<interpreter> <script>", or a
# bare executable, or -- for anything more ambitious -- a pipeline. Only the
# first two shapes can be checked by stat'ing files, and guessing about the
# third is how a working entry gets deleted. So a command with shell syntax in
# it is reported as UNCHECKED and never as broken. This tool's failure mode
# must be "said nothing", never "removed something that worked".

_SHELL_SYNTAX = ("|", "&", ";", ">", "<", "`", "$(", "&&", "||", "\n")


class Command(object):
    def __init__(self, raw):
        self.raw = raw
        self.interpreter = None      # resolved absolute path, or None if unrunnable
        self.interpreter_token = None
        self.interpreter_where = None  # where it WOULD be, for the error message
        self.script = None           # resolved absolute path, or None
        self.script_token = None
        self.unchecked = None        # why we declined to judge it


def parse_command(raw, project_dir):
    """Split a hook/statusLine command into the interpreter and the script.

    Variables are expanded only when we KNOW the value: CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR is
    what Claude Code puts in a hook's environment, and from a bare shell we can
    supply the project directory we resolved. An unexpandable variable makes the
    command unchecked, not broken -- $SOME_TOOL_HOME is very likely correct in
    the environment Claude Code actually runs the hook in.
    """
    cmd = Command(raw)
    text = str(raw or "").strip()
    if not text:
        cmd.unchecked = "the command is empty"
        return cmd
    for token in _SHELL_SYNTAX:
        if token in text:
            cmd.unchecked = ("it contains shell syntax (%r), so which file it "
                             "runs cannot be decided by looking at it" % token)
            return cmd
    try:
        import shlex
        parts = shlex.split(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        cmd.unchecked = "it does not tokenise (%s)" % exc
        return cmd
    if not parts:
        cmd.unchecked = "the command is empty"
        return cmd

    env = dict(os.environ)
    if project_dir:
        env.setdefault("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR", project_dir)
    env.setdefault("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", _claude_home())

    expanded = []
    for part in parts:
        grown = _expand(part, env)
        if grown is None:
            cmd.unchecked = ("it names an environment variable this shell does "
                             "not have set (%s)" % part)
            return cmd
        expanded.append(grown)

    # One token is the executable itself. Two or more and the first token is the
    # interpreter, and the script is the first argument that is not a flag --
    # `python3 -u hooks/x.py` is as ordinary as `python3 hooks/x.py`.
    if len(expanded) == 1:
        cmd.script_token = parts[0]
        # A lone bare name is a PATH lookup, not a relative path: `myhook` means
        # whatever the shell would find, and joining it onto the cwd would
        # invent a file nobody named.
        if _has_sep(expanded[0]):
            cmd.script = _resolve(expanded[0], project_dir)
        else:
            cmd.script = shutil.which(expanded[0]) or _resolve(expanded[0],
                                                               project_dir)
        return cmd
    cmd.interpreter_token = parts[0]
    cmd.interpreter, cmd.interpreter_where = _resolve_program(expanded[0])
    if _is_wrapper(expanded[0]):
        cmd.unchecked = ("%s is a launcher: the word after it is a subcommand "
                         "or another program, not a file, so what this ends up "
                         "running cannot be decided by looking at it"
                         % os.path.basename(expanded[0]))
        return cmd
    for token, grown in zip(parts[1:], expanded[1:]):
        if grown.startswith("-"):
            # `-c` takes code, not a path: past it there is no script to stat.
            if grown in ("-c", "-m", "-e"):
                cmd.unchecked = ("it runs inline code (%s), so there is no "
                                 "script file to check" % grown)
                return cmd
            continue
        if "=" in grown and not _has_sep(grown.split("=", 1)[0]):
            # `env A=1 python3 x.py` -- and `A=1 python3 x.py` once a shell has
            # been involved. An assignment is not the script.
            cmd.unchecked = ("it sets an environment variable inline (%s), so "
                             "the program it runs is further along than this "
                             "tool can follow" % grown.split("=", 1)[0])
            return cmd
        if not _could_be_a_script(grown):
            # THE RULE: accept the first non-flag token as the script only when
            # it CAN be one. `uv run x.py` and `npx tsx x.ts` put a subcommand
            # here, and taking it literally invented the paths `<cwd>/run` and
            # `<cwd>/tsx`, which of course do not exist -- so three working
            # registrations were reported broken, and `--all --yes`, which the
            # tool printed as the next step, deleted them. Abstaining is the
            # answer this file already has for everything undecidable.
            cmd.unchecked = ("the word after %s (%r) is not a file path, so "
                             "what this runs cannot be decided by looking at it"
                             % (os.path.basename(expanded[0]), grown))
            return cmd
        cmd.script_token = token
        cmd.script = _resolve(grown, project_dir)
        # Stop at the FIRST non-flag argument. Everything after it is an
        # argument to the program, not another program, and a missing file
        # there is the program's business -- `statusline.py --config gone.json`
        # still starts, and deleting that registration would take a working
        # status line away to report somebody else's bug.
        break
    return cmd


def _expand(text, env):
    """os.path.expandvars against a KNOWN environment, returning None when a
    variable is not in it. expandvars leaves unknown names untouched, which
    would make '$NOPE/x.py' look like a relative path that does not exist --
    a false positive, on a tool whose false positives delete configuration."""
    out = os.path.expanduser(text)
    if "$" not in out:
        return out
    import re
    pattern = re.compile(r"\$(\w+)|\$\{([^}]*)\}")
    missing = []

    def _sub(match):
        name = match.group(1) or match.group(2) or ""
        if name in env:
            return env[name]
        missing.append(name)
        return match.group(0)

    out = pattern.sub(_sub, out)
    return None if missing else out


def _resolve(path, project_dir):
    if not path:
        return None
    if os.path.isabs(path):
        return os.path.normpath(path)
    base = project_dir or os.getcwd()
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(base, path))


def _has_sep(token):
    return os.sep in token or bool(os.altsep and os.altsep in token)


#: Programs whose FIRST argument is a subcommand or ANOTHER PROGRAM, never a
#: script. Reading it as a path invents a file that was never registered, and
#: an invented missing file is a removable finding -- so this list is the
#: difference between "said nothing" and "deleted a working hook".
#:
#: _could_be_a_script() already catches most of these on its own (`run`, `tsx`
#: and `exec` are not file names), so the list is the belt for the case that
#: predicate cannot see: a prefix runner handed an ABSOLUTE interpreter path,
#: `nohup /opt/py310/bin/python3 hook.py`, where the second token looks exactly
#: like a script and a retired interpreter would read as a missing script and
#: be deleted.
#:
#: Shells are deliberately NOT here. `bash hooks/x.sh` puts the script in
#: exactly the position this file expects, and `bash -c ...` is already handled
#: as inline code -- so a missing x.sh there is the unambiguous case REMOVABLE
#: exists for, and listing bash would throw that diagnosis away.
_WRAPPERS = frozenset({
    "env", "uv", "uvx", "npx", "pnpm", "npm", "yarn", "bun", "bunx", "deno",
    "poetry", "pipenv", "pdm", "hatch", "rye", "nice", "ionice", "timeout",
    "sudo", "doas", "nohup", "stdbuf", "xargs", "command", "exec", "time",
    "jq", "flock", "setsid",
})

#: Suffixes that make a bare word (no directory in it) a plausible script name.
_SCRIPT_SUFFIXES = (".py", ".js", ".mjs", ".cjs", ".ts", ".tsx", ".sh", ".bash",
                    ".zsh", ".rb", ".pl", ".lua", ".php", ".exe")


def _is_wrapper(token):
    name = os.path.basename(token or "")
    if name.endswith(".exe"):
        name = name[:-4]
    return name in _WRAPPERS


def _could_be_a_script(token):
    """Is this token something that could name a file at all?

    Yes when it carries a directory, ends in a script suffix, or is simply
    there on disk. A bare word that is none of those -- `run`, `tsx`, `exec` --
    is a subcommand, and resolving it against the current directory is how this
    tool invented paths like `<cwd>/run` and then called them missing.
    """
    if not token:
        return False
    if _has_sep(token) or token.startswith("~"):
        return True
    if token.lower().endswith(_SCRIPT_SUFFIXES):
        return True
    return os.path.exists(token)


def _resolve_program(token):
    """(a program that will actually run, where it was looked for).

    Returns None for the first element when nothing runnable is there, and the
    caller turns that into the finding. RUNNABLE, not merely present: returning
    the normalised path for any absolute token would report
    `/nonexistent/python3 hooks/session_start.py` -- the exact shape of a pyenv
    version that was uninstalled -- as healthy. An interpreter has to exist AND
    be executable, because a shell that cannot exec it fails the hook just as
    completely as a missing file.

    A bare name is looked up on PATH exactly as the shell would, since `python3`
    can vanish as completely as an absolute path when PATH changes.
    """
    if _has_sep(token):
        path = os.path.normpath(os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(token)))
        runnable = os.path.isfile(path) and os.access(path, os.X_OK)
        return (path if runnable else None), path
    found = shutil.which(token)
    return (os.path.normpath(found) if found else None), token


def _shebang(path):
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            head = handle.read(2)
        return head == b"#!"
    except Exception:
        return False


def _is_binary(path):
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            head = handle.read(4)
        return head[:4] == b"\x7fELF" or head[:2] == b"MZ" or head[:4] == b"\xcf\xfa\xed\xfe"
    except Exception:
        return False


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# findings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: Kinds we will remove. Every one of them means the entry cannot run and the
#: thing to delete is unambiguous. The advisory kinds are NOT here on purpose: a
#: hook whose script exists but lacks +x is fixed with chmod, and deleting the
#: registration would throw away a working configuration to solve a one-command
#: problem. `malformed-root` is not here either -- "hooks" being a string is a
#: top-level key full of content nobody can interpret, and guessing at it is
#: exactly what this file must not do.
REMOVABLE = ("missing-script", "missing-interpreter", "malformed")

SEVERITY = {
    "missing-script": "broken",
    "missing-interpreter": "broken",
    "malformed": "broken",
    "malformed-root": "broken",
    "not-executable": "advisory",
    "no-shebang": "advisory",
    "script-is-directory": "advisory",
    "directory-entrypoint": "info",
    "unchecked": "info",
}


class Finding(object):
    """One thing that is wrong, and -- when it can be repaired -- exactly what
    to delete.

    `target` is the load-bearing field, and it is recorded when the finding is
    built rather than recovered later by re-parsing `where`. A regex over
    `where` matches only the deepest shape, so a malformed matcher GROUP
    produces a finding, is counted as repairable, and then plans no change at
    all -- and `--all --yes` prints 'repaired' over a file it has not touched.
    A finding that cannot say what to delete cannot claim to be repairable.

        ("entry", event, group_index, entry_index)
        ("group", event, group_index)
        ("event", event)
        ("statusline",)
        None -- report only
    """

    def __init__(self, scope, path, where, kind, detail, fix,
                 ours=False, command="", writable=True, target=None):
        self.scope = scope
        self.path = path
        self.where = where            # hooks.PreToolUse[0].hooks[0]
        self.kind = kind
        self.detail = detail
        self.fix = fix
        self.ours = ours
        self.command = command
        self.writable = writable
        self.target = target
        self.removed = False

    @property
    def severity(self):
        return SEVERITY.get(self.kind, "broken")

    @property
    def removable(self):
        return bool(self.writable and self.target and self.kind in REMOVABLE)

    def as_dict(self):
        return {"scope": self.scope, "path": self.path, "where": self.where,
                "kind": self.kind, "severity": self.severity,
                "owner": "ours" if self.ours else "other",
                "command": self.command, "detail": self.detail, "fix": self.fix,
                "target": list(self.target) if self.target else None,
                "removable": self.removable, "removed": self.removed}


def _owned_by(root, cmd):
    """Is this entry OURS?

    Ownership is decided by the path in the command, not by the name of the
    script: another tool is perfectly entitled to have a session_start.py. The
    script is the signal (the interpreter is usually somebody's pyenv), with the
    interpreter as a fallback for the case where the script could not be
    resolved at all.

    When this file has been copied out of its checkout there is no install root
    to compare against and every entry reads as another tool's. That is the
    honest answer, and main() says so out loud rather than guessing -- pass
    --root to name the checkout, or --all to act anyway.
    """
    if not root:
        return False
    root = os.path.normpath(root)
    for candidate in (cmd.script, cmd.interpreter):
        if not candidate:
            continue
        if candidate == root or candidate.startswith(root + os.sep):
            return True
    return False


def diagnose_command(sf, where, raw, root, project_dir, label, target):
    """Every way a "<interpreter> <script>" string can fail to run, as findings.

    `label` is what to call the thing in prose: 'hook' or 'statusLine'. Both
    fail identically -- a statusLine whose command is gone is the same missing
    file as a hook whose command is gone -- so they are checked by the same
    code rather than by two copies that can drift. `target` is what a repair
    would delete, and only the findings that a repair CAN act on carry it.
    """
    out = []
    cmd = parse_command(raw, project_dir)
    ours = _owned_by(root, cmd)
    if cmd.unchecked:
        out.append(Finding(sf.scope, sf.path, where, "unchecked",
                           "not checked: %s" % cmd.unchecked,
                           "look at it by hand if this %s is misbehaving" % label,
                           ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
        return out

    # The interpreter first. It is the half people forget to stat, and it fails
    # exactly as loudly as the script: an uninstalled pyenv version breaks every
    # tool call with a message about the SCRIPT's path, which sends the reader
    # to a file that is sitting right there.
    if cmd.interpreter_token is not None and cmd.interpreter is None:
        looked_in = cmd.interpreter_where or cmd.interpreter_token
        if not _has_sep(looked_in):
            why = "the interpreter %s is not on PATH" % looked_in
        elif os.path.exists(looked_in):
            why = "the interpreter %s exists but is not executable" % looked_in
        else:
            why = "the interpreter %s does not exist" % looked_in
        out.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, where, "missing-interpreter",
            "%s (the script it would run, %s, may be perfectly fine)"
            % (why, cmd.script_token or "?"),
            "install that interpreter, repoint the command at one you have, or "
            "remove the entry",
            ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable, target=target))
        return out

    if cmd.script is None:
        out.append(Finding(sf.scope, sf.path, where, "unchecked",
                           "not checked: no script path could be read out of the "
                           "command",
                           "look at it by hand if this %s is misbehaving" % label,
                           ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
        return out

    if not os.path.exists(cmd.script):
        out.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, where, "missing-script",
            "the %s it runs does not exist: %s" % (label, cmd.script),
            "restore that file, or remove the entry",
            ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable, target=target))
        return out

    if os.path.isdir(cmd.script):
        # ADVISORY, never removable. `python3 <dir>` runs <dir>/__main__.py and
        # `node <dir>` resolves <dir>/package.json main or index.js: pointing an
        # interpreter at a directory is a working invocation, not a typo.
        # Classing it as a missing script -- which is removable -- would make a
        # directory under the checkout read as OURS and delete a working hook on
        # the plain `--yes` path, with no flag at all. REMOVABLE is
        # documented to mean "the file it names is gone", and this is not that.
        runner = os.path.basename(cmd.interpreter_token or "")
        entries = []
        if runner.startswith("python"):
            entries = [os.path.join(cmd.script, "__main__.py")]
        elif runner.startswith("node") or runner.startswith("bun") \
                or runner.startswith("deno"):
            entries = [os.path.join(cmd.script, "package.json"),
                       os.path.join(cmd.script, "index.js")]
        present = [e for e in entries if os.path.exists(e)]
        if present:
            # It runs. Say so and move on -- an entry this tool can see working
            # must not be counted against the run, or a healthy machine exits
            # non-zero and the user goes looking for a problem that is not there.
            out.append(Finding(
                sf.scope, sf.path, where, "directory-entrypoint",
                "not checked further: %s is a directory, and %s runs it via %s"
                % (cmd.script, runner, os.path.basename(present[0])),
                "nothing to do -- this is a working invocation",
                ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
            return out
        hint = (" (expected %s)" % ", ".join(os.path.basename(e) for e in entries)
                if entries else "")
        out.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, where, "script-is-directory",
            "%s is a directory, and no entry point %s would run was found in "
            "it%s" % (cmd.script, runner or "it", hint),
            "put the entry point there, or repoint the command at a file -- "
            "this is NOT removed automatically, because a directory is a "
            "legitimate thing to hand an interpreter",
            ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
        return out

    # With no interpreter in front of it the script has to be able to run
    # itself. Both of these are REPORTED and never removed: the file is there,
    # the user meant to run it, and one command fixes it.
    if cmd.interpreter_token is None:
        if not os.access(cmd.script, os.X_OK):
            out.append(Finding(
                sf.scope, sf.path, where, "not-executable",
                "%s exists but is not executable, and nothing in the command "
                "would interpret it" % cmd.script,
                "chmod +x %s" % _quote(cmd.script),
                ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
        elif not _shebang(cmd.script) and not _is_binary(cmd.script):
            out.append(Finding(
                sf.scope, sf.path, where, "no-shebang",
                "%s is executable but starts with neither a #! line nor a known "
                "binary header, so what runs it is up to the shell"
                % cmd.script,
                "add a #! line (for example '#!/usr/bin/env python3'), or put the "
                "interpreter in front of it in the command",
                ours=ours, command=raw, writable=sf.writable))
    return out


def _quote(path):
    return path if all(ch not in path for ch in " \t\"'$`\\") else json.dumps(path)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# walking a settings document
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Shape is checked at every level. A settings.json is hand-edited, and the
# shapes below have all been seen: a string where the hooks list belongs, an
# entry with no "command" key, a null sitting in the list. None of them may
# raise -- a repair tool that crashes on a malformed file is a repair tool that
# does not run on the machines that need it most.

def diagnose(sf, root, project_dir):
    if sf.state != "ok":
        return

    hooks = sf.data.get("hooks")
    if hooks is not None and not isinstance(hooks, dict):
        # No target: "hooks" being a string is a top-level key holding content
        # nobody can interpret, and this tool does not delete top-level keys it
        # cannot read. Reported, never removed, not even under --all.
        sf.findings.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, "hooks", "malformed-root",
            "\"hooks\" is a %s; Claude Code expects an object of event name -> "
            "list" % type(hooks).__name__,
            "fix it by hand -- this is not something to guess at",
            ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable))
        hooks = None

    if isinstance(hooks, dict):
        for event, groups in hooks.items():
            if not isinstance(groups, list):
                sf.findings.append(Finding(
                    sf.scope, sf.path, "hooks.%s" % event, "malformed",
                    "the %s entry is a %s; Claude Code expects a list of matcher "
                    "groups" % (event, type(groups).__name__),
                    "fix it by hand, or remove the event with --all",
                    ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
                    target=("event", event)))
                continue
            for gi, group in enumerate(groups):
                where_group = "hooks.%s[%d]" % (event, gi)
                if not isinstance(group, dict):
                    sf.findings.append(Finding(
                        sf.scope, sf.path, where_group, "malformed",
                        "this matcher group is a %s, not an object"
                        % type(group).__name__,
                        "fix it by hand, or remove the group with --all",
                        ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
                        target=("group", event, gi)))
                    continue
                entries = group.get("hooks")
                if not isinstance(entries, list):
                    sf.findings.append(Finding(
                        sf.scope, sf.path, where_group + ".hooks", "malformed",
                        "the group's \"hooks\" is a %s, not a list"
                        % type(entries).__name__,
                        "fix it by hand, or remove the group with --all",
                        ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
                        target=("group", event, gi)))
                    continue
                for ei, entry in enumerate(entries):
                    where = "%s.hooks[%d]" % (where_group, ei)
                    target = ("entry", event, gi, ei)
                    if not isinstance(entry, dict):
                        sf.findings.append(Finding(
                            sf.scope, sf.path, where, "malformed",
                            "this hook entry is a %s, not an object"
                            % type(entry).__name__,
                            "fix it by hand, or remove the entry with --all",
                            ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
                            target=target))
                        continue
                    raw = entry.get("command")
                    if not isinstance(raw, str) or not raw.strip():
                        sf.findings.append(Finding(
                            sf.scope, sf.path, where, "malformed",
                            "this hook entry has no usable \"command\" (%s)"
                            % json.dumps(raw),
                            "fix it by hand, or remove the entry with --all",
                            ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
                            target=target))
                        continue
                    sf.findings.extend(diagnose_command(
                        sf, where, raw, root, project_dir, "hook script", target))

    status = sf.data.get("statusLine")
    if status is None:
        return
    if not isinstance(status, dict):
        sf.findings.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, "statusLine", "malformed",
            "\"statusLine\" is a %s, not an object" % type(status).__name__,
            "fix it by hand, or remove it with --all",
            ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
            target=("statusline",)))
        return
    raw = status.get("command")
    if not isinstance(raw, str) or not raw.strip():
        sf.findings.append(Finding(
            sf.scope, sf.path, "statusLine", "malformed",
            "\"statusLine\" has no usable \"command\" (%s)" % json.dumps(raw),
            "fix it by hand, or remove it with --all",
            ours=False, command="", writable=sf.writable,
            target=("statusline",)))
        return
    sf.findings.extend(diagnose_command(
        sf, "statusLine", raw, root, project_dir, "statusLine program",
        ("statusline",)))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# repairing a settings document
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def plan_repair(sf, findings):
    """Build the repaired document, and describe every change in one line each.

    MARK, THEN SWEEP -- never pop by index while walking. Popping entries in
    reverse index order is correct for entries alone but silently wrong the
    moment a group or a whole event is also being removed: every pending index
    behind it shifts. Collecting the deletions first and rebuilding each list
    once cannot get that wrong, and it makes "which container did WE empty"
    answerable, which is the other half of the contract.

    An emptied container goes with its contents -- a group with no hooks left,
    then an event with no groups left -- but only when this run emptied it. A
    matcher group that was already empty before we arrived is somebody's
    deliberate placeholder and none of our business.

    The one container never removed is "hooks" itself. It is a top-level key the
    user wrote, and taking a top-level key away is a bigger surprise than
    leaving an empty object behind; the output says so rather than doing it
    quietly.
    """
    import copy
    document = copy.deepcopy(sf.data)
    changes = []

    drop_statusline = None
    drop_events = set()                  # event
    drop_groups = set()                  # (event, group_index)
    drop_entries = {}                    # (event, group_index) -> {entry_index}
    reason = {}                          # where -> the finding, for the change line
    for finding in findings:
        target = finding.target
        if not target:
            continue
        reason[finding.where] = finding
        if target[0] == "statusline":
            drop_statusline = finding
        elif target[0] == "event":
            drop_events.add(target[1])
        elif target[0] == "group":
            drop_groups.add((target[1], target[2]))
        elif target[0] == "entry":
            drop_entries.setdefault((target[1], target[2]), set()).add(target[3])

    def why(where, fallback):
        finding = reason.get(where)
        if finding is None:
            return fallback
        return _clip(finding.command or finding.detail, 76)

    hooks = document.get("hooks")
    if isinstance(hooks, dict):
        for event in list(hooks):
            if event in drop_events:
                hooks.pop(event, None)
                changes.append("remove the malformed hook event hooks.%s  (%s)"
                               % (event, why("hooks.%s" % event, "malformed")))
                continue
            groups = hooks.get(event)
            if not isinstance(groups, list):
                continue
            kept = []
            emptied_by_us = False
            for gi, group in enumerate(groups):
                where_group = "hooks.%s[%d]" % (event, gi)
                if (event, gi) in drop_groups:
                    changes.append("remove the malformed matcher group %s  (%s)"
                                   % (where_group,
                                      why(where_group,
                                          why(where_group + ".hooks", "malformed"))))
                    emptied_by_us = True
                    continue
                wanted = drop_entries.get((event, gi))
                if wanted and isinstance(group, dict) and \
                        isinstance(group.get("hooks"), list):
                    entries = group["hooks"]
                    for ei in sorted(wanted):
                        if 0 <= ei < len(entries):
                            where = "%s.hooks[%d]" % (where_group, ei)
                            changes.append("remove %s  (%s)"
                                           % (where, why(where, "broken")))
                    group["hooks"] = [entry for ei, entry in enumerate(entries)
                                      if ei not in wanted]
                    if not group["hooks"]:
                        changes.append("remove the now-empty matcher group %s"
                                       % where_group)
                        emptied_by_us = True
                        continue
                kept.append(group)
            if kept:
                hooks[event] = kept
            elif emptied_by_us:
                hooks.pop(event, None)
                changes.append("remove the now-empty hook event hooks.%s" % event)
            else:
                hooks[event] = kept

    if drop_statusline is not None:
        document.pop("statusLine", None)
        changes.append("remove \"statusLine\"  (%s)" % drop_statusline.detail)

    if isinstance(document.get("hooks"), dict) and not document["hooks"]:
        changes.append("note: \"hooks\" is now an empty object. It is left in "
                       "place -- removing a top-level key you wrote is not this "
                       "tool's call.")
    return document, changes


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# writing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def detect_indent(text):
    """The indent the file already uses, so a repair does not reformat it.

    json.dumps cannot preserve everything -- key order it can (dicts keep
    insertion order), indentation it can if we measure it, and comments,
    trailing commas and blank-line grouping it cannot, because json never
    parsed them in the first place. The caller says all of that out loud.
    """
    for line in text.splitlines()[1:]:
        stripped = line.lstrip(" \t")
        if not stripped or stripped[0] in "}]":
            continue
        lead = line[:len(line) - len(stripped)]
        if lead.startswith("\t"):
            return "\t"
        if lead:
            return len(lead)
        break
    return 2


def render(document, original_text):
    """The repaired document as text -- or ValueError rather than bad JSON.

    allow_nan=False is the whole guard. Left at its default, json.dumps emits
    the bare tokens `NaN` / `Infinity` / `-Infinity`, which are Python's
    extension to JSON and are rejected by Node -- so the default would let this
    tool write a settings.json that Claude Code cannot read while every check
    it runs (all of them Python) says the file is fine. Raising here means the
    caller's existing failure path refuses the write instead.
    """
    indent = detect_indent(original_text) if original_text else 2
    out = json.dumps(document, indent=indent, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False)
    # Match the file's own ending. A settings.json that had no trailing newline
    # should not grow one just because it was repaired.
    if not original_text or original_text.endswith("\n"):
        out += "\n"
    return out


def _reject_constant(name):
    raise ValueError("the token %s is not JSON that Claude Code can read" % name)


def strict_loads(text):
    """json.loads under NODE's rules, not Python's.

    Python's json is a superset: it accepts NaN, Infinity and -Infinity, which
    JSON.parse rejects. Verifying our own output with the reader that produced
    it is circular -- it proved only that Python agrees with Python, which is
    how `Infinity` reached a user's settings.json under a "verified on disk"
    line. Raises ValueError on anything Node would refuse.
    """
    return json.loads(text, parse_constant=_reject_constant)


def _node_binary():
    """A node interpreter to check our output with, if the machine has one.

    Never required. This file's contract is that it runs with nothing but the
    standard library on a machine that is already broken, so node is used when
    it happens to be there and its absence is not a failure -- strict_loads()
    is the check that always runs.
    """
    for name in ("node", "nodejs"):
        found = shutil.which(name)
        if found:
            return found
    return None


def _node_accepts(path):
    """(verdict, detail) from a real JSON.parse, or (None, why) if none ran.

    This is the only verification in the file that is not performed by the
    program that wrote the bytes, which is the point of it.
    """
    node = _node_binary()
    if not node:
        return None, "no node interpreter on PATH"
    import subprocess
    script = ("var fs=require('fs');"
              "try{JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(process.argv[1],'utf8'));"
              "console.log('OK')}"
              "catch(e){console.log('ERR '+e.message.split('\\n')[0]);"
              "process.exitCode=1}")
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([node, "-e", script, path],
                              stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                              timeout=20)
    except Exception as exc:
        return None, "could not run node (%s: %s)" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
    reply = (done.stdout or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
    if done.returncode == 0 and reply.startswith("OK"):
        return True, os.path.basename(node)
    return False, reply[3:].strip() or "JSON.parse refused it"


def next_backup(path):
    """settings.json.bak-1, then .bak-2 ... never reusing a number.

    A timestamp would be prettier and would also let two runs in the same second
    overwrite each other. A counter that stops at the first unused name cannot
    destroy an existing backup, which is the only property that matters here.
    """
    counter = 1
    while True:
        candidate = "%s.bak-%d" % (path, counter)
        # lexists, not exists: exists() follows symlinks, so a DANGLING backup
        # name read as "free" and the copy was then written THROUGH the link,
        # landing wherever it pointed while the output said ~/.claude. The
        # backup is the safety net; it has to be where the run says it is.
        if not os.path.lexists(candidate):
            return candidate
        counter += 1


def atomic_write(path, text):
    """Temp file in the same directory, fsync, os.replace.

    Same directory because os.replace is only atomic within a filesystem, and
    atomic because the alternative -- open(path, 'w') -- truncates the user's
    settings the instant it is called. An interrupt one line later would then
    leave an empty settings.json, which is the failure this whole file exists to
    undo.
    """
    directory = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)) or "."
    handle, temp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=".oe-repair.", dir=directory)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(handle, "w", encoding="utf-8") as out:
            out.write(text)
            out.flush()
            os.fsync(out.fileno())
        if os.path.exists(path):
            shutil.copystat(path, temp)
        os.replace(temp, path)
        temp = None
    finally:
        if temp and os.path.exists(temp):
            try:
                os.unlink(temp)
            except Exception:
                pass


def untouched_keys(before, after, managed):
    """Every top-level key we did not claim must be identical, and in order.

    Serialised with sort_keys so that a nested reordering is not mistaken for a
    change, and compared as a LIST so that a reordering at the top level is.
    This is the check that turns "we only removed the broken entry" from a
    claim into something the run can prove and print.
    """
    problems = []
    before_keys = [k for k in before if k not in managed]
    after_keys = [k for k in after if k not in managed]
    if before_keys != after_keys:
        lost = [k for k in before_keys if k not in after_keys]
        gained = [k for k in after_keys if k not in before_keys]
        if lost:
            problems.append("KEYS LOST: %s" % ", ".join(lost))
        if gained:
            problems.append("unexpected new keys: %s" % ", ".join(gained))
        if not lost and not gained:
            problems.append("key ORDER changed: %s -> %s"
                            % (before_keys, after_keys))
    for key in before_keys:
        if key in after_keys and json.dumps(before[key], sort_keys=True) != \
                json.dumps(after[key], sort_keys=True):
            problems.append("key modified: %s" % key)
    return problems


#: v1.0.0 diagnoses only. See "WHY THIS BUILD DOES NOT WRITE" above.
WRITES_ENABLED = False


def write_repair(sf, document, changes):
    """Back up, write, read back, and roll back if the result is not what we meant.

    Order is the contract: nothing is written until the file on disk is still
    the file we diagnosed, the rendered text is provably valid, a backup exists
    AND has been re-read and proved byte-identical to the original. A backup we
    did not verify is not a backup, it is a hope.
    """
    if not WRITES_ENABLED:
        # Reached only if a caller bypassed the check in main(). Refusing here
        # too is the point: this function deletes hook registrations, and the
        # failure mode of the code that decides WHICH ones is a machine on
        # which no tool call works.
        raise RuntimeError(
            "write_repair() called in a diagnose-only build; refusing to "
            "modify %s" % sf.path)
    # -- 0. which file are we actually writing? -----------------------------
    #
    # A ~/.claude/settings.json symlinked into a dotfiles repo is an ordinary
    # layout, and os.replace() on the link destroys it: the link becomes a
    # regular file, the dotfiles copy keeps the broken hook, and the next
    # `stow`/`chezmoi apply` puts the breakage back -- after this tool said it
    # was fixed. The .bak-N cannot undo that, because it is a copy of the
    # CONTENT and what was lost is the file's identity. So resolve once and
    # repair the file the link points at.
    target = sf.path
    if os.path.islink(sf.path):
        real = os.path.realpath(sf.path)
        if not os.path.exists(real):
            fail("%s is a symlink to %s, and nothing is there. Repairing it "
                 "would replace your link with a regular file; fix the link "
                 "first. Nothing was written." % (sf.path, real))
            return None, 1
        target = real
        say("  symlink     %s" % sf.path)
        say("              -> writing %s, so the link survives" % target)
        say("              (commit that file if it is under version control)")

    # -- 1. is the file still the one we diagnosed? -------------------------
    #
    # Everything below was planned against the snapshot load() took at the
    # start of the run. An editor saving settings.json, `/hooks` in a surviving
    # Claude Code session, or a second oe-repair with different flags all land
    # inside that window -- and without this check their edit is silently
    # overwritten while the run prints "verified on disk: N/N other top-level
    # key(s) byte-identical", a claim about a file that no longer exists.
    try:
        with open(target, "rb") as handle:
            current = handle.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        fail("%s has been deleted since this run started. Nothing was written "
             "-- run this again." % target)
        return None, 1
    except Exception as exc:
        fail("could not re-read %s before writing (%s: %s); refusing to write."
             % (target, type(exc).__name__, exc))
        return None, 1
    if current != sf.raw:
        fail("%s changed while this was running, so the repair was planned "
             "against a file that is no longer there. Nothing was written -- "
             "run this again." % target)
        return None, 1

    # -- 2. render BEFORE the backup ---------------------------------------
    #
    # render() refuses to emit Python's non-JSON tokens, and a refusal must
    # cost the user nothing: no backup file, no touched settings.
    try:
        text = render(document, sf.text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        fail("cannot rewrite %s safely: %s. Nothing was written." % (target, exc))
        return None, 1
    try:
        strict_loads(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        fail("the repaired text would not be valid JSON (%s). This is a bug in "
             "oe-repair. Nothing was written." % exc)
        return None, 1

    # -- 3. backup ----------------------------------------------------------
    backup = None
    if os.path.exists(target):
        backup = next_backup(target)
        try:
            shutil.copy2(target, backup)
            with open(backup, "rb") as handle:
                copied = handle.read()
            with open(target, "rb") as handle:
                original = handle.read()
        except Exception as exc:
            fail("could not take a backup of %s (%s); refusing to write."
                 % (target, exc))
            return None, 1
        if copied != original:
            fail("the backup at %s does not match the original byte for byte; "
                 "refusing to write." % backup)
            return None, 1
        say("  backup      %s" % backup)

    # -- 4. write -----------------------------------------------------------
    try:
        atomic_write(target, text)
    except Exception as exc:
        fail("write failed: %s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
        _rollback(target, backup)
        return backup, 1

    # -- 5. verify, and NOT with the reader that wrote it -------------------
    #
    # Read back from disk, not from memory: the thing being verified is the
    # FILE, and "the variable I just built is the variable I just built" proves
    # nothing about what landed. strict_loads applies Node's rules rather than
    # Python's, and where a node interpreter exists it gets the last word --
    # the only check here the writer did not perform on itself.
    try:
        with open(target, "r", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            written = strict_loads(handle.read())
    except Exception as exc:
        fail("could not re-read what we wrote (%s: %s)" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
        _rollback(target, backup)
        return backup, 1

    node_ok, node_detail = _node_accepts(target)
    if node_ok is False:
        fail("the file we wrote is rejected by JSON.parse (%s), which is the "
             "parser Claude Code reads settings with." % node_detail)
        _rollback(target, backup)
        return backup, 1

    managed = {"hooks", "statusLine"}
    problems = untouched_keys(sf.data, written, managed) \
        if isinstance(written, dict) else ["the written file is not a JSON object"]
    if isinstance(written, dict) and written != document:
        problems.append("the file on disk is not the document we planned")
    if problems:
        fail("WROTE A FILE THAT FAILS VERIFICATION:")
        for problem in problems:
            fail("    " + problem)
        _rollback(target, backup)
        return backup, 1

    preserved = [k for k in sf.data if k not in managed]
    say("  wrote       %s" % target)
    say("  verified on disk: %d/%d other top-level key(s) byte-identical and in "
        "the original order" % (len(preserved), len(preserved)))
    if node_ok:
        say("  verified by %s: JSON.parse reads it -- so Claude Code will too"
            % node_detail)
    else:
        say("  (%s, so the JSON check was this tool's own strict reader only)"
            % node_detail)
    return backup, 0


def _rollback(path, backup):
    if not backup or not os.path.exists(backup):
        fail("NO BACKUP TO RESTORE. %s is as the failed write left it." % path)
        return
    try:
        shutil.copy2(backup, path)
        fail("ROLLED BACK: restored %s from %s" % (path, backup))
    except Exception as exc:
        fail("COULD NOT RESTORE %s from %s: %s: %s"
             % (path, backup, type(exc).__name__, exc))
        fail("your original settings are intact at %s -- copy it back by hand."
             % backup)


def _clip(text, width):
    text = " ".join(str(text).split())
    return text if len(text) <= width else text[:width - 1] + "\u2026"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def collect_files(args):
    """Every settings file Claude Code merges, in the order it merges them.

    Not one file: an install can land in any of the three writable scopes, and a
    broken hook in the project scope breaks that checkout just as completely as
    one in the user scope. The managed/policy files are appended read-only --
    they are an administrator's artifact, so they are found and reported and
    never edited.
    """
    if args.settings:
        return [SettingsFile("--settings",
                             os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(args.settings)))]
    home = _claude_home()
    files = [SettingsFile("user", os.path.join(home, "settings.json")),
             SettingsFile("user-local", os.path.join(home, "settings.local.json"))]
    project = _project_dir(args.project)
    if project:
        files.append(SettingsFile(
            "project", os.path.join(project, ".claude", "settings.json")))
        files.append(SettingsFile(
            "project-local", os.path.join(project, ".claude", "settings.local.json")))
    for directory in _policy_dirs():
        files.append(SettingsFile(
            "managed", os.path.join(directory, "managed-settings.json"),
            writable=False))
        dropin = os.path.join(directory, "managed-settings.d")
        try:
            names = sorted(os.listdir(dropin))
        except Exception:
            names = []
        for name in names:
            if name.endswith(".json") and not name.startswith("."):
                files.append(SettingsFile("managed", os.path.join(dropin, name),
                                          writable=False))
    seen, unique = set(), []
    for sf in files:
        key = os.path.normpath(sf.path)
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        unique.append(sf)
    return unique


def report_file_state(sf):
    """The one-line inventory row, plus the detail for a file we cannot use."""
    label = "  %-14s %s" % (sf.scope, sf.path)
    note = "  (read only -- administrator settings)" if not sf.writable else ""
    if sf.state == "missing":
        say(label + c("   not present", "2") + note)
        return
    if sf.state == "ok":
        hooks = sf.data.get("hooks")
        events = len(hooks) if isinstance(hooks, dict) else 0
        bits = ["%d hook event(s)" % events]
        if sf.data.get("statusLine") is not None:
            bits.append("statusLine")
        bits.append("%d other key(s)"
                    % len([k for k in sf.data if k not in ("hooks", "statusLine")]))
        say(label + c("   " + ", ".join(bits), "2") + note)
        return
    say(label + c("   " + sf.state.upper(), "31;1") + note)
    error = sf.error or {}
    if sf.state == "unparseable":
        say("      not valid JSON: %s" % error.get("message"))
        source = error.get("source")
        if error.get("empty"):
            # An empty file is the commonest shape of this and the least
            # obvious: it is not "settings with nothing in them", it is a syntax
            # error, and pointing at line 1 column 1 of nothing helps nobody.
            say("      the file is EMPTY, and an empty file is not valid JSON. The "
                "smallest")
            say("      valid settings file is the two characters:  {}")
        elif error.get("line"):
            say("      at line %s, column %s:" % (error.get("line"),
                                                  error.get("column")))
            if source is not None:
                say("        %s" % source.rstrip("\n"))
                column = error.get("column") or 1
                say("        %s^" % (" " * max(0, column - 1)))
        say(c("      This file is NOT repairable automatically. Rewriting a file we "
              "cannot", "33"))
        say(c("      parse would mean claiming we preserved settings we never read.",
              "33"))
        if error.get("empty"):
            say(c("      Put {} in it, or move it aside:", "33"))
        else:
            say(c("      Fix the line above by hand, or move the file aside:", "33"))
        say("        cp %s %s" % (_quote(sf.path), _quote(next_backup(sf.path))))
    elif sf.state == "not-an-object":
        say("      %s" % error.get("message"))
        say(c("      This file is NOT repairable automatically -- there is nothing "
              "in it", "33"))
        say(c("      shaped like settings to repair. Move it aside:", "33"))
        say("        cp %s %s" % (_quote(sf.path), _quote(next_backup(sf.path))))
    else:
        say("      %s" % error.get("message"))


def render_finding(finding):
    marker = {"broken": ("x", "31;1"), "advisory": ("!", "33"), "info": ("\u00b7", "2")}
    glyph, colour = marker.get(finding.severity, ("x", "31;1"))
    owner = "OURS" if finding.ours else "another tool"
    say("  " + c(glyph, colour) + " %s  [%s]  %s" % (finding.where, owner, finding.scope))
    say("      command: %s" % _clip(finding.command, 88))
    say("      %s" % finding.detail)
    say("      fix:     %s" % finding.fix)


def main(argv=None):
    global _QUIET
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="oe-repair",
        description="Find and remove Claude Code hook/statusLine registrations "
                    "that point at files which do not exist. This build "
                    "DIAGNOSES ONLY and never writes.",
        epilog="Runs by absolute path with nothing installed: "
               "python3 <checkout>/bin/oe-repair")
    parser.add_argument("project", nargs="?",
                        help="the project directory whose .claude/settings.json "
                             "should be checked (default: the nearest ancestor of "
                             "the current directory that has one)")
    parser.add_argument("--yes", action="store_true",
                        help="ACCEPTED AND REFUSED in this build: it explains "
                             "why and still writes nothing. See the module "
                             "docstring.")
    parser.add_argument("--all", action="store_true",
                        help="also remove broken entries belonging to other tools. "
                             "Off by default: silently deleting somebody else's "
                             "integration is worse than reporting it.")
    parser.add_argument("--root",
                        help="the Overwatch Enforcer checkout, for deciding which "
                             "entries are ours (default: the parent of the "
                             "directory holding this script)")
    parser.add_argument("--settings",
                        help="check exactly this one file and nothing else")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", dest="as_json",
                        help="print the verdict as JSON on stdout and nothing else "
                             "(diagnostics still go to stderr)")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    _QUIET = bool(args.as_json)

    # GUARD ONE OF TWO. write_repair() refuses independently, because a single
    # guard on a path that can delete a working hook is one edit away from
    # being gone. Neither guard trusts the other.
    _refused = bool(args.yes)
    args.yes = False

    root = args.root or os.environ.get("OE_ROOT") or _default_root()
    root = os.path.normpath(os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(root)))
    # Does this root look like a checkout at all? When oe-repair has been copied
    # somewhere else -- or when the tree it lived in was gutted -- nothing can be
    # attributed to it, and saying so beats an ownership answer nobody can check.
    root_is_checkout = any(os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, name))
                           for name in ("install.py", "hooks", "oe", "bin"))
    project = _project_dir(args.project)

    say()
    say(c("  oe-repair", "1") + c("  --  Claude Code settings, checked and repaired "
                                  "from outside Claude Code", "2"))
    say()
    say("  install root   %s%s" % (root, "" if root_is_checkout else
                                   c("   (does not look like a checkout)", "33")))
    say("  project        %s" % (project or c("none found", "2")))
    say()
    say(c("  settings files Claude Code merges:", "2"))

    files = collect_files(args)
    for sf in files:
        sf.load()
        report_file_state(sf)
        diagnose(sf, root if root_is_checkout else None, project)

    if not root_is_checkout:
        say()
        warn("this copy of oe-repair is not inside an Overwatch Enforcer checkout, "
             "so no")
        warn("  entry can be attributed to it. Everything below is reported as "
             "another")
        warn("  tool's. Pass --root <checkout> to attribute, or --all to act anyway.")

    exit_code = 0
    findings = [f for sf in files for f in sf.findings]
    fatal = [sf for sf in files if sf.state in ("unparseable", "unreadable",
                                                "not-an-object")]
    broken = [f for f in findings if f.severity == "broken"]
    advisory = [f for f in findings if f.severity == "advisory"]
    info = [f for f in findings if f.severity == "info"]

    if findings:
        say()
        say(c("  findings:", "2"))
        for finding in broken + advisory + info:
            render_finding(finding)

    # What we are allowed to remove, per file. `--all` widens ownership; it does
    # not widen the KINDS, so an unparseable file stays unrepairable and a
    # chmod-able script stays reported rather than deleted.
    targets = {}
    unrewritable = []
    for sf in files:
        chosen = [f for f in sf.findings
                  if f.removable and (f.ours or args.all)]
        if chosen and not sf.rewritable:
            # Diagnosed, printed, and deliberately not repaired. See
            # _rewrite_blocker(): the alternative is a rewrite this tool cannot
            # prove Claude Code will still be able to read.
            unrewritable.append(sf)
            continue
        if chosen:
            targets[id(sf)] = (sf, chosen)

    for sf in unrewritable:
        say()
        say(c("  %s  %s" % (sf.scope, sf.path), "1"))
        say(c("    NOT rewritten: %s" % (sf.rewrite_block or {}).get("message"),
              "33"))
        say(c("    The entries above are real and worth fixing; edit this file "
              "by hand.", "33"))
        exit_code = 1

    declined = [f for f in findings
                if f.removable and not f.ours and not args.all]
    # Broken entries in a managed/policy file. They can be diagnosed and they
    # cannot be repaired here, and the difference has to be SAID: without this
    # the report showed two red crosses, planned nothing for them, and left the
    # reader to wonder why the tool had skipped the only entries it had just
    # called broken.
    administrator = [f for f in findings
                     if not f.writable and f.severity == "broken"]

    repairs = []

    if not targets and not fatal and not broken and not advisory:
        say()
        say(c("  nothing wrong. No file was written.", "32"))
    elif not targets and declined:
        # NOT the same case as "nothing is repairable", and saying so cost a
        # user the one sentence that would have kept them looking. `targets` is
        # empty whenever every removable finding was declined on OWNERSHIP
        # grounds -- the normal case for a third-party hook, and the guaranteed
        # case when this script has been copied out of its checkout. The
        # entries below are removable; they are simply not ours to remove.
        say()
        say("  %d broken entr%s found, none of them attributable to this tool."
            % (len(declined), "y" if len(declined) == 1 else "ies"))
        say("  They ARE removable -- see the declined block below.")
    elif not targets and not unrewritable:
        say()
        say("  nothing here is automatically repairable. No file was written.")

    for sf, chosen in targets.values():
        document, changes = plan_repair(sf, chosen)
        say()
        say(c("  %s  %s" % (sf.scope, sf.path), "1"))
        # A plan that changes nothing must not reach the writer: a finding that
        # claims to be repairable but plans no change would still take a backup,
        # rewrite the file (reformatting it), and print "repaired" over
        # something it had not fixed. The planner and the finding disagreeing is
        # a bug in THIS file, so say so plainly rather than writing anything.
        if not [line for line in changes if not line.startswith("note:")]:
            say("    nothing to change here after all -- reported above, not "
                "repaired.")
            say("    (a finding claimed to be repairable and planned no change; "
                "that is a bug in oe-repair)")
            exit_code = 1
            continue
        for change in changes:
            say("    - " + change)
        if sf.text and render(sf.data, sf.text) != sf.text:
            say()
            warn("this file's exact formatting is not what json.dumps produces. "
                 "Key order")
            warn("  and indentation are preserved; comments, trailing commas and "
                 "blank-line")
            warn("  grouping are not -- json never saw them. The .bak-N taken "
                 "before the")
            warn("  write keeps the original to diff against.")
        if not args.yes:
            continue
        backup, code = write_repair(sf, document, changes)
        repairs.append({"path": sf.path, "backup": backup,
                        "changes": changes, "ok": code == 0})
        if code:
            exit_code = 1
        else:
            for finding in chosen:
                finding.removed = True

    if administrator:
        say()
        say(c("  left alone -- ADMINISTRATOR settings, which this tool never "
              "edits:", "33"))
        for finding in administrator:
            say("    %s in %s" % (finding.where, finding.path))
        say("    A managed/policy settings file belongs to whoever administers "
            "this")
        say("    machine. Send them the lines above; editing it here would be "
            "undone")
        say("    by the next policy push anyway.")

    if declined:
        say()
        say(c("  left alone -- these belong to another tool:", "33"))
        for finding in declined:
            say("    %s in %s" % (finding.where, finding.path))
        say("    Read the commands above first. Removing them is a manual "
            "edit in this")
        say("    build -- delete the entr%s named above from the file%s named "
            "above." % ("y" if len(declined) == 1 else "ies",
                        "" if len({f.path for f in declined}) == 1 else "s"))

    if fatal:
        exit_code = 1
    if any(f.severity in ("broken", "advisory") and not f.removed for f in findings):
        exit_code = 1

    if targets:
        say()
        say(c("  DIAGNOSE ONLY -- nothing was written.", "1"))
        say("    Every change above is listed line by line. Apply them by hand:")
        say("    open the file, remove the entries named, and start Claude Code")
        say("    again. Take a copy first.")
    if _refused:
        say()
        say(c("  --yes was refused.", "33"))
        say("    This build does not repair. The repair path can delete a hook")
        say("    that is WORKING -- it resolves $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR and relative")
        say("    paths against the directory you are standing in, and it reads")
        say("    `uv run x.py` and `npx tsx x.ts` as a missing script. Removing")
        say("    a live PreToolUse entry stops every tool call in every session")
        say("    on this machine, which is worse than the fault above.")

    if exit_code == 0 and repairs:  # unreachable while WRITES_ENABLED is False
        say()
        say(c("  repaired. Start Claude Code again.", "32"))
    say()

    if args.as_json:
        payload = {
            "tool": "oe-repair",
            "schema": VERSION,
            "root": root,
            "root_is_checkout": root_is_checkout,
            "project": project,
            # `applied` is always false in this build. `writes_enabled`
            # distinguishes "you did not pass --yes" from "this build cannot
            # write at all", which a machine reader otherwise cannot tell.
            "applied": bool(args.yes),
            "writes_enabled": WRITES_ENABLED,
            "yes_refused": _refused,
            "include_other_tools": bool(args.all),
            "files": [{"scope": sf.scope, "path": sf.path, "state": sf.state,
                       "writable": sf.writable, "rewritable": sf.rewritable,
                       "rewrite_block": sf.rewrite_block, "error": sf.error}
                      for sf in files],
            "findings": [f.as_dict() for f in findings],
            "repairs": repairs,
            "exit": exit_code,
        }
        _emit(sys.stdout, json.dumps(payload, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False))
    return exit_code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        # Interrupted BEFORE any write is the normal case (the report is the
        # slow part), and os.replace makes an interrupt during the write a
        # no-op on the original file. Either way, say which.
        fail("interrupted. Nothing was written by this run unless a 'wrote' line "
             "appeared above.")
        sys.exit(130)
